netlink: NETLINK_GET_STRICT_CHK and the filters of dumps

What:
- NETLINK_GET_STRICT_CHK is a flag of a netlink socket that setsockopt(2)
  sets and getsockopt(2) reads, as net/netlink/af_netlink.c keeps
  NETLINK_F_STRICT_CHK (a value shorter than an int clears it; reading it
  into less than an int is EINVAL), instead of ENOPROTOOPT.
- With it, an address dump has only the addresses of the interface that
  ifa_index names, and a route dump only the routes of RTM_F_CLONED (none:
  netstack has no route cache), the table (rtm_table or RTA_TABLE), the
  protocol, the type and the output interface (RTA_OIF) it names, as
  net/ipv4/devinet.c:inet_dump_ifaddr(), net/ipv6/addrconf.c:inet6_dump_addr()
  and net/ipv4/fib_frontend.c:ip_valid_fib_dump_req() take them; an
  interface that does not exist ends the dump with ENODEV.
- With or without it, a link dump has only the links whose master IFLA_MASTER
  names (-1 for none) and whose kind IFLA_LINKINFO's IFLA_INFO_KIND names (of
  the kinds gVisor creates, bridge and veth), as
  net/core/rtnetlink.c:rtnl_dump_ifinfo() does.
- NLM_F_DUMP_FILTERED marks, as in Linux, the messages of a link dump with a
  filter (not its NLMSG_DONE), the messages and NLMSG_DONE of an address
  dump filtered by ifa_index (cb->answer_flags), and, under strict checking,
  every message of a route dump, as Linux dumps no route exceptions then,
  and NLMSG_DONE when the request has a filter (ip_valid_fib_dump_req()). A
  dump that fails ends with its error in the payload of NLMSG_DONE, as
  Linux's netlink_dump() sends dump_done_errno, instead of always 0.

Why: netavark 1.17 sets NETLINK_GET_STRICT_CHK on every netlink socket and
fails without it (src/network/netlink.rs: "Needs to be enabled for dump
filtering to work"): "netavark: host netlink socket: IO error: Protocol not
available (os error 92)". netavark of every version finds out whether a
bridge has ports left, when a container leaves it, with a link dump filtered
by IFLA_MASTER, and removes the bridge when there is none; gVisor returned
every link, so no bridge was ever removed. netavark 1.17 also reads a
bridge's addresses with a dump filtered by ifa_index.

Not done: Linux rejects, under strict checking, a dump request whose header
has values it does not filter on or whose attributes it does not know
(EINVAL); gVisor takes them as before. A table that does not exist is an
empty dump rather than ENOENT.

Upstream: none found for these (upstream PRs 15949 and 16090, in
release-20261109.0, made dumps honor the requested family); drop this patch
when upstream has them.

Verified:
- The new syscall tests NetlinkRouteTest.GetStrictCheck, .GetLinkDumpFilters,
  .GetAddrDumpFilteredByIndexWithStrictCheck and
  .GetRouteDumpFilteredByOifWithStrictCheck (the dumped entries and the
  NLM_F_DUMP_FILTERED of messages and NLMSG_DONE) pass on Linux 7.0 under runc
  and on runsc with this patch, under the runtime arguments of runsc and of
  runsc-nested, and fail on ALE's 0.1.2. The whole socket_netlink_route_test
  passes on runsc (126 tests), as does
  //pkg/sentry/socket/netlink/route:route_test.
- netavark 1.17.2 (Podman 5.8.9, Fedora 43) in a sandbox under runsc-nested
  opened its sockets and set up its bridge; it and netavark 1.4.0 (Podman
  4.9.3, Ubuntu 24.04) removed their bridge when its last container stopped,
  as on Linux.

